Unless the bees conspire to hide their numbers, and carefully hover in place
(making them trivial to hit, which they're not), I don't think one bee could
block another.

> However, A bee could block out an umber hulk 10 spaces away, due to the
>fact that objects appear smaller when far away.

A goblin would only block an umber hulk from a worm's-eye view. If you're
looking from above the goblin's head, it'll never block the umber hulk.
Pretty much the only monsters that are significantly taller than the player
are giants, isn't that right? I suppose minotaurs are a little taller.

The thing neglected in mere height considerations is that when one's in
combat with a monster, you and it are going to be dodging around enough
to be able to see what's behind it. Some monsters are big and wide, like
minotaurs, but I think you're always going to be able to see around an elf.
So I'm of a mind to make the sizes be Normal, Hulking, and Giant. Normal
things don't block the light, Hulking things do and also block sight of
things behind them, except of Giants (which of course also block).

To program, change the boulder code so that giants are visible behind them,
and make Hulking and Giant things act like boulders w.r.t. vision.
